Superficial thermal burn from brief contact with oven rack to forearm
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Linear demarcated erythema, Tense clear blister formation, Momentary hot metal contact

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-039
Reason for Visit: Acute localized thermal burn with rapid blister formation requiring assessment of depth and application of sterile barrier dressings.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute hot metal contact burn causing extreme pain and blistering on the upper limb. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to explicitly rule out full-thickness dermal necrosis, safely decompress any large tense blisters, and apply a specialized occlusive burn dressing to halt severe neuropathic pain before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I accidentally grazed my arm on the hot oven rack, and it instantly formed a painful, tight blister.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of a transient, momentary graze against an active oven rack 30 minutes ago. This resulted in a sharply defined linear area of erythema corresponding precisely to the shape of the heated metal wire. The patient experienced immediate, intense localized burning pain upon physical contact. There is rapid formation of a thin, tense blister. The patient reports exquisite sensitivity to ambient air and light physical touch.
Functional Status: Fully independent; guarding the forearm against any contact with clothing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hazard in the kitchen; patient advised on using long oven mitts.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/76 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, in acute localized distress, holding the affected forearm elevated and away from the body.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals a 1x5cm linear demarcated erythema on the volar forearm with tense, clear blister formation. There is blanching of the erythematous borders upon applied gentle pressure. Complete absence of deep white, leathery charring or insensate eschar. Normal limb function is maintained with intact neurovascular status distal to the burn site. Swelling is restricted firmly to the immediate peri-burn tissue. The blister roof is currently intact, providing a natural biological dressing over the exquisitely sensitive dermis.
Environmental Observations: Oven is off and secure.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Correctly applied cool running water for 15 minutes post-injury.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial thermal burn to forearm - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Full-thickness thermal burn - Ruled out due to: Severe pain upon light touch (intact nerve endings), presence of fluid-filled blistering, and brisk marginal capillary refill.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Immediate acute thermal mechanism and absence of spreading, warm induration or systemic fever.

Deep partial-thickness burn - Ruled out due to: Rapid formation of tense, clear blisters rather than sluggishly blanching, fixed dermal staining.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Direct contact with heated household elements frequently causes focal superficial partial-thickness burns. Bedside clinical evaluation definitively assessed the burn depth as superficial, confirming intact deep dermal perfusion and sensation. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to rule out full-thickness injury and to immediately apply specialized sterile hydrogel/silicone dressings. This rapid intervention instantly suppresses the intense, air-induced pain cycle and prevents environmental contamination of the compromised epidermal barrier, safely avoiding an emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The intact blister was deliberately preserved to act as a sterile biological dressing and reduce pain. Cleaned the surrounding skin with sterile saline. Applied a specialized non-adherent silicone foam dressing to protect the blister from accidental mechanical shearing. Tetanus prophylaxis verified.
Positive Behavioral Change: Do not purposefully burst or drain the blister yourself. Leaving the blister intact ensures the raw, sensitive skin underneath remains in a perfectly sterile, fluid-filled environment, which completely stops the burning pain and prevents infection while the new skin grows.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Silicone foam dressing.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 3-5 days to check wound healing and change the dressing if the blister has naturally ruptured.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or your local GP if the redness rapidly spreads away from the burn line, if the area becomes intensely swollen and hot, or if thick, foul-smelling yellow pus develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The blister may spontaneously rupture despite the protective dressing; this is a normal progression of the burn.
Clinical Course: The sharp burning will cease completely within hours under the dressing. The blister fluid will slowly reabsorb, and the dead skin will slough off over 7 to 10 days, leaving a pink, sensitive scar that will fade over months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you notice red lines traveling up your arm toward your armpit, or if you develop a sudden fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The specialized dressing acts as a shock absorber. It stops your clothes from rubbing against the delicate blister, preventing it from tearing open and exposing the painful nerve endings beneath.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing completely dry in the shower. Use a plastic bag over the arm if necessary. Buy long oven mitts for future cooking.

